course: dictionary
lesson: 11258
title: Wow and its many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Wow» is a small word with several jobs. Said on its own, often with a big voice, it shows you are amazed or surprised. Read the words around it to be sure what it means.
explain: In informal talk, «wow» can also be a verb or a noun. «She wowed the judges» means she impressed them greatly. «The show was a wow» means the show was a great success.
explain: Tone matters. «Wow, thanks a lot» can mean the opposite of amazed: the speaker is annoyed or not impressed. The clues are in how and why it is said, just as with any other word.
explain: People have their own words for amazement too. «Sheesh» means wow, and «eish» can mean wow or oh no, depending on the situation. Context tells you which one is meant.
fact: «Wow» is an exclamation, said when you are surprised, amazed or impressed.
fact: As a verb, «wow» means to impress someone greatly, as in «She wowed the judges».
fact: As an informal noun, «a wow» means a great success.
fact: Sarcastic «wow» hides annoyance: «Wow, thanks a lot» does not mean thank you.
fact: «Wow words» are interesting words that make writing exciting.
exercise: «Wow! Look at that rainbow!» What does wow show here? | Anger | *Amazement | Tiredness
exercise: «She wowed the judges with her dance.» What does wowed mean? | Made the judges tired | *Impressed the judges greatly | Argued with the judges | Ran away from the judges
exercise: «Wow, thanks a lot», said Sam, rolling his eyes. What does the sarcastic wow show? | *Sam is annoyed or not impressed | Sam is amazed by the gift | Sam is very happy | Sam is asking a question
test: «The school play was a wow, and everyone clapped.» What is a wow here? | A broken chair | A type of costume | A loud noise | *A great success
test: Which word would be a good wow word in a story? | went | said | *dazzling | nice
exam: Which sentence uses wow as an exclamation? | *«Wow, the fireworks are huge!» | «She wowed the crowd.» | «The show was a wow.»
